                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I got this game back in the day when I bought the NEOGEO bundle for the Metal Slug games, and while I did play some of it back in 2019 or so (even beating the story mode), I left it gathering dust until this year, when I decided to go for 100% completion.\n\nWell, let's say you shouldn't make the same mistake I did. See, a lot of reviews I read about the game mention how fun it is as a versus game, and while that might be true, the experience against the AI is miserable. See, the game has four difficulty modes, going from easy to insane, but they might as well all be called insane, as from the third stage onwards, difficulty ramps up significantly. While there's only seven stages in the game, the final two are particularly frustrating, as the AI has almost perfect accuracy in their playstyle.\n\nNow, before moving forward in my criticism of the game, let me talk a bit about the gameplay. It's a versus 2D, top-down scrolling shooter. Sounds kinda weird, but it's an interesting mix of mechanics: you've got your screen, your opponent's got theirs, enemies show up and you shoot them. If you make chains (as enemies explode when killed, potentially killing other enemies), you then send out attacks to your enemy, which they can bounce back and vice versa. You can also summon special, unblockable attacks by charging your shots or reversing enemy attacks.\n\nAll in all, sounds like a fun idea. The issue lies, however, in the amount of crap that quickly starts bouncing your way, or the opponent's way. When there's a lot of stuff on screen (and there will be a lot of stuff), not only can you not see for shit, you also get slowdowns, which can mess up your movement. On top of that, if an enemy hits you (not an attack), you get knocked out and start bouncing around while desperately pressing inputs in order to regain control. You do become invincible when in that state, but as soon as you regain control, invincibility ends, so you could end up hit again, either by an enemy or an attack. Add to that the AI's almost perfect response in the final stages, and you can see how it's a recipe for frustration. Beating the game at that point feels more like depending on RNG than any sort of strategy.\n\nBeyond that, the game has a cute style, reminiscent of '80s or '90s magical girl anime, although the English translation leaves a lot to be desired; there's lots of broken English. It does have a good soundrack, though. As for the story, it's nothing to write home about, just a very simple story about an evil lord trying to conquer the world (funnily enough, it was with that character that I managed to do an almost perfect arcade run).\n\nI don't really recommend it.",
